  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+14y+45.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 7y+45\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${7}^{2},$ writing the constant term $45$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 7y+{7}^{2}}_{\u23df}-\\,{7}^{2}+45\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+7{)}^{2}-{7}^{2}+45\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+7{)}^{2}-4.\n$$",
